TEXT 72 288 Left 2 ;-1/2*[(a - b)^2 - a^2 - b^2] = a*b\n \nVery fast, but there are 6 elements\nthat perform a quadratic operation,\neven if they work natively. It could be\nreduced as in "pwr(A,2).asc", to only\n4 quadratics, but with 4 extra linear,\nat the same costs as in that example.
TEXT 608 288 Left 2 ;-1/4*[(a - b)^2 - (a + b)^2] = a*b\n \nOnly 4 quadratic operations, plus\nan E-source, which shouldn't cause\nheadaches since it's permanently\ngrounded, isolated from I/O, and it\nonly performs a sign inversion.
TEXT 424 -144 Left 2 ;-(a - b)^2
TEXT 336 -8 Left 2 ;a^2
TEXT 336 152 Left 2 ;b^2
TEXT 864 -8 Left 2 ;(a + b)^2
TEXT 944 -152 Left 2 ;-(a - b)^2
TEXT 160 -496 Left 2 ;Faster than B-source with V(a)*V(b), but\nstill slow due to the behavioural nature of\nthe resistor. Deleting the ground at the\noutput slows down everything. Prone to\n"timestep too small", particularly when\nused in feedback.
TEXT 1120 288 Left 2 ;This is a very fast, A*B, but it's not perfect. V(a*b_alt) uses the behavioural\nresistor, which, like all B-sources, is slow as the numbers go high (hundreds or more).\nTo convert it, I1 is used as a unity current generator, which makes the voltage across\nS1 to be the same as the reference voltage due to the negative feedback. V/I=R, I=1,\nthus V=R. The bandwidth is given by the loop filter, but also the amount of distortion.\nThe maximum swing is given by Ron and Roff. For I=1, the middle point is kept 1,\nthus Ron are symmetrical around 1. Without V3 and E1, the multiplier would only work\nwith positive values. To accomodate, V3 sets the estimated absolute maximum value\nof the reference voltage, V(a), plus a tiny bit more, while E1 accomodates the voltage\nswing after the final operation. Instead of these, two resistors with the same *negative*\nvalues could have been used, but these, while tolerated by LTspice, might cause\nproblems.\n Disadvantages:\n- the need to estimate the minimum voltage of one of the inputs -- {comp};\n- the estimated bandwidth of one of the input signals, which directly influences the\namount of error from the feedback.\nAdvantages:\n- possibly the fastest multiplier;\n- no B-sources.
TEXT 1128 -208 Left 2 ;R(S1) = V(res)/I(I1) = V(res)/1A == V(a)
TEXT -472 568 Left 2 ;.save v(a*b_v1a) V(a*b_v2)\n.opt plotwinsize=0 numdgt=15 nomarch
TEXT 784 576 Left 2 ;The winner.
